e to pass flatus, fever, green or yellow-green vomit\n  Favours second: abdominal bloating, confusion, frequent urination, inability to defecate, loss of appetite\n\n[RAG context for 'A 55-year-old man presents to the emergency department with nausea and vomiting for the past week' (score=0.65):]\n  Source: Adhesive small bowel obstruction - an update. > History and Physical\n  On physical examination, the patient may appear uncomfortable or anxious due to pain, with signs of dehydration such as dry mucous membranes and tachycardia, especially in the case of persistent vomit\n[RAG context for 'A 55-year-old man presents to the emergency department with nausea and vomiting for the past week' (score=0.66):]\n  Source: Fecal incontinence in older patients. A narrative review. > History and Physical\n  Patients with fecal impaction often give a history of inability to evacuate stools spontaneously and complain of total constipation. In most instances, an associated history of progressive abdominal d\n[RAG context for 'He decided to come in when his symptoms worsened' (score=0.68):]\n  Source: Adhesive small bowel obstruction - an update. > History and Physical\n  Abdominal distension, due to the accumulation of gas and fluids above the obstruction, is common and may be particularly noticeable in high or complete obstructions. Changes in bowel movements, includ\n[RAG context for 'He decided to come in when his symptoms worsened' (score=0.70):]\n  Source: Adhesive small bowel obstruction - an update. > History and Physical\n  On physical examination, the patient may appear uncomfortable or anxious due to pain, with signs of dehydration such as dry mucous membranes and tachycardia, especially in the case of persistent vomit\n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  constipation → chronic constipation leading to fecal impaction → Fecal Impaction with Superimposed Constipation\n    (frequency: frequent)\n    Suggestion: Assess for signs of fecal impaction (e.g., 